# The build context is the repository root (`docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .`),
# and everything in it is uploaded to the Docker daemon before the first
# instruction runs - whether or not the Dockerfile ever COPYs it.
#
# That matters twice over here. A local checkout accumulates .mypy_cache,
# htmlcov and a .venv, which is tens to hundreds of megabytes of nothing; and it
# accumulates .ironflow/state.db, data/curated/ and logs/, which is run history
# and processed data. Against a remote or CI daemon that data leaves the machine
# for no reason at all.
#
# So this file denies everything and then re-admits exactly what the Dockerfile
# copies. It fails closed, which is the same posture the rest of the platform
# takes: adding a COPY to the Dockerfile means adding its exception below, and
# forgetting to breaks the build loudly instead of shipping something silently.

*

!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!LICENSE
!src/

# Re-excluded inside the tree admitted above: build metadata and bytecode from
# the host would otherwise be baked into the wheel and the image layer, and a
# host-built .egg-info can shadow the one the image builds for itself.
src/**/__pycache__/
src/**/*.py[cod]
src/*.egg-info/
